{"id": "policy-procedure-for-retention-and-destruction-o", "code": "RTFMR", "title": "POLICY & PROCEDURE ON RETENTION TIME FOR MEDICAL RECORDS", "html": "<p class=\"lead\"><b>POLICY &amp; PROCEDURE FOR </b></p><p class=\"lead\"><b>RETENTION AND DESTRUCTION OF MEDICAL RECORDS</b></p><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>PURPOSE:</b></li></ul><p>To provide guidelines on the review process, retention period and destruction of documents related to Protected Health Information.</p><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>SCOPE:</b></li></ul><p>Medical Record department</p><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>RESPONSIBILITY:</b></li></ul><p>Medical Record Officer</p><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>ABBREVIATIONS:</b></li><ul class=\"d\"><li>HMIS:Hospital Management Information System</li><li>IA:Initial Assessment</li><li>ICU:Intensive Care Unit</li><li>IMS:Information Management System</li><li>IP:Indoor Patient</li><li>IT:Information Technology</li><li>MLC:Medico-Legal Case</li><li>MR:Medical Records</li><li>MRD:Medical Records Department</li><li>NABH:National Accreditation Board For Hospitals and Healthcare Providers</li><li>OP:Outdoor Patient</li><li>SOP:Standard Operating Procedures</li><li>UHID:Unique Hospital Identification Number</li></ul><li><b>DEFINITIONS:</b></li></ul><p>Nil</p><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>REFERENCE:</b></li><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>NABH</b><b>:</b> NABH Standards for Hospitals</li></ul><li><b>POLICY &amp; PROCEDURES:</b></li><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>Statutory Requirements:</b></li></ul></ul><p>Complied with the local laws and regulations: Code of medical ethics 2002; Consumer protection act 1986.</p><ul class=\"d\"><ul class=\"d\"><li><b>Retention period of records:</b></li><ul class=\"d\"><li>Timeline:</li></ul></ul></ul><table class=\"t\"><tr><th><b>Type of Record</b></th><th><b>Retention Period</b></th></tr><tr><td>Out Patient Records</td><td>03 Years</td></tr><tr><td>Inpatient Records</td><td>05 Years</td></tr><tr><td>MLC (Medico Legal Case) Record</td><td>Life time or till the final decision at the court of Law</td></tr><tr><td>Medical record of death cases</td><td>Life time</td></tr><tr><td>Medical record of pediatrics patients</td><td>Till the patient become 21 years old [18 years +3 Years]</td></tr><tr><td>Birth Register</td><td>Life time</td></tr><tr><td>Death Register</td><td>Life time</td></tr><tr><td>Casualty Register</td><td>Life time</td></tr><tr><td>MTP Register</td><td>05 years from the end of the calendar year</td></tr><tr><td>OT Register</td><td>30 years</td></tr><tr><td>PNDT Act (Section 29 &amp; Rule – 9.6)</td><td>02 years</td></tr><tr><td>BMW Act</td><td>05 years</td></tr><tr><td>Income Tax Act</td><td>08 years</td></tr><tr><td>ICMR Guidelines – ART</td><td>10 years – National Registry</td></tr></table><ul class=\"d\"><ul class=\"d\"><ul class=\"d\"><li>Medical considerations are the primary basis for deciding how long to retain medical records.</li><li>In all cases, medical records should be kept for at least as long as the length of time of the statute of limitations for medical malpractice claims. The statute of limitations may be three or more years, depending on the state law. State medical associations and insurance carriers are the best resources for this information.</li><li>Whatever the statute of limitations, MRD personnel should measure time from the last professional contact with the patient.</li><li>If a patient is a minor, the statute of limitations for medical malpractice claims may not apply until the patient reaches the age of majority. Immunization records always must be kept.</li><li>In order to preserve confidentiality when discarding old records, all documents should be destroyed.</li><li>Before discarding old records, patients should be given an opportunity to claim the records or have them sent to another physician, if it is feasible to give them the opportunity.</li></ul><li><b>Destruction of old records: </b></li><ul class=\"d\"><li>The records which are to be destroyed as per the policy shall be identified in the October / November month of every year.</li><li>Outpatient records after 3 years of the last visit by patient and Inpatient records after 5 years of the last visit by patient shall be identified from the Inactive records.</li><li>Their UHID number and the patient details like Patient’s name, Age, Sex and Place shall be collected.</li><li>The details shall be forwarded to the Medical Director and an approval letter is obtained from them.</li><li>The information shall be published in all the national newspapers before 45 days of destruction, mentioning the last date for clarifications if any.</li><li>In case any of the patients asks for a copy of the record before the last date mentioned in the newspaper, they are provided with the same.</li><li>If not, the records sent for shredding to the approved vendor &amp; certificate of complete destruction is taken from vendor.</li><li>The details of all the records that are destroyed shall be noted in the appropriate register.</li><li>All medical records of pediatric cases will be preserved till the child attains 18 years of age and cannot be destroyed even after 3 yrs.</li><li>Indoor case papers of all patients who are currently undergoing treatment at our hospital will be kept separate.</li><li>Confidentiality cannot be jeopardized because of an inadequate method of destruction.</li><li>To ensure that the medical records are completely destroyed, the Medical Records Officer should supervise their destruction</li><li><b>Disposal of films:</b></li><ul class=\"d\"><li>X-Ray films of the documents to be disposed are sold for scrap dealers after removing the identity of patients on the films.</li><li>The films of medico legal cases are kept with the MRD under lock and key permanently.</li></ul></ul><li>Common destruction methods are:</li><ul class=\"d\"><li>Shredding, pulping, and pulverizing for paper records.</li><li>Pulverizing for microfilm or microfiche, laser discs, document imaging applications.</li><li>Magnetic degaussing for computerized data.</li><li>Shredding or cutting for DVDs.</li><li>Demagnetizing magnetic tapes.</li></ul><li>Medical offices should maintain documentation of the destruction of health records which include the following:</li><ul class=\"d\"><li>Date of destruction</li><li>Method of destruction</li><li>Description of the disposed records</li><li>Inclusive dates</li><li>A statement that the records were destroyed in the normal course of business</li><li>The signatures of the individuals supervising and witnessing the destruction</li></ul></ul></ul><p><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"\">Flow Chart:</p>"}